CORE broken-smt-backend
array_of_structs_loop1.sv
--bound 1
^\[main\.p0\] always \(main\.lp == 6 \|-> !main\.nq\[0\]\.hold_preq && main\.nq\[1\]\.hold_preq && main\.nq\[2\]\.hold_preq && main\.nq\[3\]\.hold_preq\): PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p1\] always \(main\.lp == 200 \|-> !main\.nq\[0\]\.hold_preq && main\.nq\[1\]\.hold_preq && !main\.nq\[2\]\.hold_preq && !main\.nq\[3\]\.hold_preq\): PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p2\] always main\.nq\[2\]\.pseq == 100 && main\.nq\[1\]\.tag == 2'b10: PROVED up to bound 1$
^\[main\.p3\] always main\.nq == main\.nq2: PROVED up to bound 1$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A loop over a packed array of packed structs that reads and writes members
of the elements. The RTL builder substituted the composed value of the
entire array into every read of a member, and the lowering replicated
struct and array values once per member or element when flattening them;
together, this grew exponentially with the number of iterations and ran
out of memory for four iterations.

The test is excluded from the SMT back-ends: the equality of two packed
arrays (p3) is encoded as the equality of two SMT arrays indexed by 64-bit
values, of which only the used indices are constrained, and hence is
refuted by Z3 and cvc5 although the printed values agree. This happens
independently of this test's fix, and is to be addressed in the SMT2
back-end.
